  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "J5Y4dLeMomRfJMs31qa76tiAt8s=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:11.266Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Kainos is approved on 15 lots across 8 Crown Commercial Service frameworks, which the workbook records as the strongest documented public-sector access it holds on any firm. The wording matters: this is evidenced access, upgraded from the assumption that a large UK supplier probably has framework coverage, to a counted position on named frameworks.\n\nThe offsetting finding is that its anchor is Workday \u2014 it is described as Europe's largest boutique Workday partner, alongside Microsoft and AWS credentials including Premier MSP status and 500-plus certified engineers. That anchoring dilutes custom-build purity: a practice organised around configuring a proprietary SaaS suite is a different delivery motion from bespoke, client-owned systems, and it is the SaaS vendor that owns the system of record in those engagements.\n\nThe row stays at priority 9 despite the best access in the corpus, and that inversion is the transferable lesson. Access to public buyers is necessary but not sufficient; the screen weights partnership fit above route-to-market, the same way it does for the Sopra Steria entries. Framework coverage tells you a firm can sell into government, not that it would sell this.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Kainos (UK)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> FRAMEWORK ACCESS NOW EVIDENCED: approved on 15 lots across 8 Crown Commercial Service frameworks - the strongest documented public-sector access in the workbook. Workday anchoring dilutes custom purity.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:26:11.266563Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:11.266567Z"
    }
  },
